(4017797, 570051, 39057, '<td valign="top">Hello,<br/> <br/>I have been noticing that your "drop" photos have been taken at 1/500 sec. I know you use flash and according to specs, you can''t go faster than 1/500 while using your flash.<br/> <br/>Did you know that there is a way to use higher than 1/500 speed while using your flash? I think you may like to experiment with 1/1000 or 1/2000 speed to get even better "drop" shots!<br/> <br/>Check out the shot below. It was done with my D70 and SB800 at 1/2000 sec.<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=383011" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/39057/thumb/383011.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/39057/thumb/383011.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/39057/thumb/383011.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>PM me and I''ll give you the simple info on how to work it out.<br/> <br/>Kenskid<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hello, I have been noticing that your "drop" photos have been taken at 1/500 sec. I know you use flash and according to specs, you can''t go faster than 1/500 while using your flash. Did you know that there is a way to use higher than 1/500 speed while using your flash? I think you may like to experiment with 1/1000 or 1/2000 speed to get even better "drop" shots! Check out the shot below. It was done with my D70 and SB800 at 1/2000 sec.  PM me and I''ll give you the simple info on how to work it out. Kenskid ', '2007-08-21 16:41:52.000000', NULL, 0),
