(4025900, 569825, 16488, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Macros of insects are fun to look at and a challenge to make well. What I like most about macros is the way they give me leisure to study the details of small creatures. I like the textures in their bodies and wings. So, to me, it''s important that those details are crisp and the whole image is enjoyable to view for a period of time.<br/> <br/>Here I have to agree with comments about the overexposure. A quick look at this image''s histogram proves the point. Enhancing the darker tones would help to bring out the details.<br/> <br/>I also find this a little too yellow. It may be that the leaf was close to that color but it''s just not appealing. A little desaturation on the green and yellow hues would make this easier to spend time with. It would help enhance the reddish tones present in the grasshopper''s eyes and wings.<br/> <br/>The composition is okay. I''d prefer to see a bit more of the hopper''s body but that''s just my personal preference. In general I like the subject''s placement within the frame.<br/> <br/>The factor I think holds this image back the most, however, is the lack of focus on the eye. Perhaps that could be fixed in post-process...but I doubt it. It''s really important to get the eyes of animals and humans tack sharp when shooting. It''s just human nature to look at the eyes first and repeatedly as the viewer explores the image.<br/> <br/>Overall, I think it''s a nice grab shot. It''s hard to impress DPC viewers with anything less than a near-perfect bug shot since there have been so many in challenges on the site. That may be true with other subjects as well...we''re always looking for a little extra to surprise us.<br/> <br/>Congratulations on making it through your first challenge without too many scars.<br/>Keep shooting!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Macros of insects are fun to look at and a challenge to make well. What I like most about macros is the way they give me leisure to study the details of small creatures. I like the textures in their bodies and wings. So, to me, it''s important that those details are crisp and the whole image is enjoyable to view for a period of time. Here I have to agree with comments about the overexposure. A quick look at this image''s histogram proves the point. Enhancing the darker tones would help to bring out the details. I also find this a little too yellow. It may be that the leaf was close to that color but it''s just not appealing. A little desaturation on the green and yellow hues would make this easier to spend time with. It would help enhance the reddish tones present in the grasshopper''s eyes and wings. The composition is okay. I''d prefer to see a bit more of the hopper''s body but that''s just my personal preference. In general I like the subject''s placement within the frame. The factor I think holds this image back the most, however, is the lack of focus on the eye. Perhaps that could be fixed in post-process...but I doubt it. It''s really important to get the eyes of animals and humans tack sharp when shooting. It''s just human nature to look at the eyes first and repeatedly as the viewer explores the image. Overall, I think it''s a nice grab shot. It''s hard to impress DPC viewers with anything less than a near-perfect bug shot since there have been so many in challenges on the site. That may be true with other subjects as well...we''re always looking for a little extra to surprise us. Congratulations on making it through your first challenge without too many scars.Keep shooting!', '2007-08-24 11:37:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(4025982, 570592, 29011, '<td valign="top">Cool idea with the spark trails :) <br/>Shot doesn''t look too dark to me.  The exposure itself looks about right. Any brighter and the bright spots of the fire might have been over exposed. You can always brighten up the shot a bit in an image editor. Shot could''ve used some more depth of field though, and then you could''ve used a longer exposure. But these trials are really neat , a longer exposure would be something fun to play with, getting the right balance of those trials and not too many of them to crowd the shot either.</td>', 'Cool idea with the spark trails :) Shot doesn''t look too dark to me.  The exposure itself looks about right. Any brighter and the bright spots of the fire might have been over exposed. You can always brighten up the shot a bit in an image editor. Shot could''ve used some more depth of field though, and then you could''ve used a longer exposure. But these trials are really neat , a longer exposure would be something fun to play with, getting the right balance of those trials and not too many of them to crowd the shot either.', '2007-08-24 12:16:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(4026082, 570597, 26194, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Critique by psart:<br/> <br/>Wonderful Shot !!!<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/> <br/>I love the way you framed this image. Positioning of the trees in relation to fawn size is right on.  Framing is exactly what I would of done in this instance. Any closer and tree trunks would of distracted from subject. So well done on composition.<br/> <br/>Focus:<br/> <br/>Focus is alittle bit soft on fawn do to camera lens capturing grass in front of subject but it still works here since a softer image brings out the helplessness of fawn. Great job.<br/> <br/>Lighting:<br/> <br/>Lighting is alittle harse on trees in highlights, but I stress only alittle. Image still should of scored higher in my opinion.<br/> <br/>Overall:<br/> <br/>A high scoring image I would of rated you between a 7-8 on this submission. Keep up the good work and you''ll make the top placement in the near future. You have a good eye for total image capture and it will eventually pay off. <br/> <br/>Dennis (psart) if you have any questions PM me</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Critique by psart: Wonderful Shot !!! Composition: I love the way you framed this image. Positioning of the trees in relation to fawn size is right on.  Framing is exactly what I would of done in this instance. Any closer and tree trunks would of distracted from subject. So well done on composition. Focus: Focus is alittle bit soft on fawn do to camera lens capturing grass in front of subject but it still works here since a softer image brings out the helplessness of fawn. Great job. Lighting: Lighting is alittle harse on trees in highlights, but I stress only alittle. Image still should of scored higher in my opinion. Overall: A high scoring image I would of rated you between a 7-8 on this submission. Keep up the good work and you''ll make the top placement in the near future. You have a good eye for total image capture and it will eventually pay off.  Dennis (psart) if you have any questions PM me', '2007-08-24 13:08:19.000000', NULL, 0),
